{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And you shall measure the boundary of your land.</b> The Land of Israel is four hundred parasang by four hundred parasang, and it is concluded in the second chapter of Makkot: 'and you shall measure' — that they be divided into thirds: one hundred parasang from the south to Hebron, one hundred from Hebron to Shechem, one hundred from Shechem to Kedesh, and one hundred from Kedesh to the north. And it asks: what is different about this side, that they are far, and what is different about this side, that they are near? — meaning that those who dwell in the south wind and in the north wind were far from the cities of refuge one hundred parasang, while those who dwell between Hebron and Shechem and between Shechem and Kedesh were near the cities of refuge. And it answers: Abbaye said — because in Shechem murderers are numerous, as it is written: ״שם ירצחו שכמה מאי חבר כהנים״. Rabbi Elazar said: they would band together to kill persons, like priests who band together to take terumah in the threshing floors. And because murderers were prevalent there, it [Shechem] was near the towns between it and Hebron and between it and Kedesh, so that those who kill unwittingly from the towns adjacent to them would come to the refuge, and the intentional killers within them would be killed. And if you ask: if so, that in the Land of Israel murderers are prevalent, the difficulty returns to its place — why in Transjordan three etc.? And one must say: at least in Gilead it is more prevalent, for it is called עקובה מדם."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:07.925794091Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}